  "prediction": "The report discusses the historical development of the Holman rule in the House of Representatives, which establishes the separation of authorization and appropriation legislation. The rule, which can be traced back to 1835, prohibits provisions changing existing law from being included in appropriations bills except for retrenchments that reduce expenditures. The rule has undergone several amendments since its inception, with the most significant one occurring in 1876, which expanded the definition of retrenchments to include the reduction of federal officials' salaries and compensation. The report also highlights how the rule has been interpreted through precedents, which have tended to narrow its application over time. For instance, retrenchments must result in an obvious reduction of funds in the bill and cannot be contingent on an event or apply to funds other than those appropriated in the pending general appropriations bill. The report concludes by noting that the Holman rule is not intended to open the door for legislative provisions that would expand the scope of the bill and that its application is subject to the general requirement for germaneness. The report also mentions that during the 115th Congress, a separate order was adopted to expand the definition of retrenchments to include the reduction of the number and salary of federal officers and the compensation of any person paid out of the Treasury. However, the applicability of this separate order was not adopted for the 116th Congress.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report discusses the historical development of the Holman rule in the House of Representatives, which establishes the separation of authorization and appropriation legislation. The rule, which can be traced back to 1835, prohibits provisions changing existing law from being included in appropriations bills except for retrenchments that reduce expenditures. The rule has undergone several amendments since its inception, with the most significant one occurring in 1876, which expanded the definition of retrenchments to include the reduction of federal officials' salaries and compensation. The report also highlights how the rule has been interpreted through precedents, which have tended to narrow its application over time. For instance, retrenchments must result in an obvious reduction of funds in the bill and cannot be contingent on an event or apply to funds other than those appropriated in the pending general appropriations bill. The report concludes by noting that the Holman rule is not intended to open the door for legislative provisions that would expand the scope of the bill and that its application is subject to the general requirement for germaneness. The report also mentions that during the 115th Congress, a separate order was adopted to expand the definition of retrenchments to include the reduction of the number and salary of federal officers and the compensation of any person paid out of the Treasury. However, the applicability of this separate order was not adopted for the 116th Congress.",
  "score": 38.84892036424098,
  "answers": [
    "Although congressional rules establish a general division of responsibility under which questions of policy are kept separate from questions of funding, House rules provide for exceptions in certain circumstances. One such circumstance allows for the inclusion of legislative language in general appropriations bills or amendments thereto for \"germane provisions that retrench expenditures by the reduction of amounts of money covered by the bill.\" This exception appears in clause 2(b) of House Rule XXI and is known as the Holman rule, after Representative William Holman of Indiana, who first proposed the exception in 1876. Since the period immediately after its initial adoption, the House has interpreted the Holman rule through precedents that have tended to incrementally narrow its application. Under current precedents, for a legislative provision or amendment to be in order, the legislative language in question must be both germane to other provisions in the measure and must produce a clear reduction of appropriations in that bill. In addition, the House adopted a separate order during the 115th Congress that provided for retrenchments of expenditures by a reduction of amounts of money covered by the bill to be construed as applying to: any provision or amendment that retrenches expenditures by\u2014 (1) the reduction of amounts of money in the bill; (2) the reduction of the number and salary of the officers of the United States; or (3) the reduction of the compensation of any person paid out of the Treasury of the United States. This separate order was not readopted for the 116th Congress. This report provides a history of this provision in House rules and an analysis of precedents that are illustrative of its possible application."
  ],
